  "state": {
    "case_id": "28",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their 70s presented with worsening shortness of breath, chest tightness, and episodes of palpitations. Medical history was remarkable for hypertension and kidney insufficiency.\n\nInitial evaluation revealed:- Heart rate: 53 beats/min- Blood pressure: 221/85 mm Hg\n\n- Laboratory tests:  * B-type natriuretic peptide: 838.6 pg/mL (normal level, <300 pg/mL)- ECG findings:  * Initial ECG:    Image Title: Initial 12-lead ECG    Image Description: Shows sinus bradycardia with prolonged P-wave duration (180 milliseconds), biphasic P wave morphology in leads II, III, and aVF, PR interval 280 milliseconds, QRS duration 120 milliseconds, rsR pattern in lead V1  * Follow-up ECG:    Image Description: Shows atrial flutter- Echocardiography:  * Left atrial enlargement (52 mm)  * Normal left ventricular wall thickness  * Mild pulmonary hypertension\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Atrial flutter with bradycardia\nB. Bayés syndrome (Advanced interatrial block with paroxysmal atrial arrhythmias)\nC. Atrial Flutter with Variable Block\nD. Sick sinus syndrome\n",
